Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Infra Fiber Bundles", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Abstract written by Pavel Hajek on 25 October 2026.", \
"TI"]], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Infra fiber bundles are ",
 StyleBox["minimal models of fibrations on graphs",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". Unlike lattice gauge theory, which usually uses continuous or discrete \
labels for parallel transport on edges and gauge transformations at vertices, \
infra fiber bundles operate with ",
 StyleBox["unlabeled graphs",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " and a vertex projection, and all structure is derived from the fibers and \
their connectivity."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "It turns out that there are natural minimal models of ",
 StyleBox["sections, connections and holonomy",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", and that certain natural properties make sense even at the ",
 StyleBox["infra-level",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", where all the information is specified by binary vertex connectivity and \
the projection. We have to relax some conditions because the objects are \
generally only partially defined. Surprisingly, many constructions still \
work, and whether they are globally defined becomes a separate question. We \
also build ",
 StyleBox["scale dependence",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " into our construction to study different properties at different observer \
scales."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We use inert objects such as ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraFiberedGraph"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraFiberedGraph", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["projection", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "08934054-f391-4419-9681-3a9621cedf54"],
 ". ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["RandomInfraSection"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/RandomInfraSection", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["fib", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["partial", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["k", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "beaa3e65-2573-4c27-8bb2-cd568417f85f"],
 " samples sections extending a prescribed partial section, and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["RandomInfraConnection"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/RandomInfraConnection", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["fib", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["k", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "68e67681-d470-40a1-bc4a-a25af7242f81"],
 " samples complete connections. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraHolonomy"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraHolonomy", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["fib", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["connection", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["loop", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "62c4a8ba-6348-4827-91b8-962b02a30347"],
 " gives the map from a fiber to itself obtained by transport around a closed \
base walk."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "There are many natural examples of fibered graphs. For example, a connected \
",
 StyleBox["directed graph can be seen as a fibered graph over a path graph",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " when coordinatized by shortest-path distance from its set of sources. In \
InfraAnalysis, such graphs are viewed as \"puffed\" real lines. ",
 StyleBox["Graph coordinatization",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " - assigning labels to vertices based on natural choices and constructions, \
such as shortest-path projections onto a chosen set of orthogonal paths - \
gives another set of examples. When coordinatization is based on observer \
chains, as in the Wolfram Physics Project, fibers can be understood as ",
 StyleBox["internal degrees of freedom",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", which are not visible to mechanical observers."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "There are natural constructions of ",
 StyleBox["tangent and cotangent fiber bundles",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " at a given observer scale r: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraTangentBundle"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraTangentBundle", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["r", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "de0e4bed-aa53-4ad1-8d97-fea3b36cfe49"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraCotangentBundle"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraCotangentBundle", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["r", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "c05191b5-4bca-43c1-b93e-2589279b26c0"],
 ". These can be used to define infra notions of a ",
 StyleBox["Levi-Civita connection",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " and a ",
 StyleBox["canonical 1-form",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", respectively. Whereas the canonical 1-form is an exact construction with \
a unique result, the Levi-Civita connection need not be uniquely defined. We \
also distinguish between rays, which take into account the internal \"webbing\
\" of the fibers, and their endpoints, called ",
 StyleBox["displacements",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". These give different bundles related by a bundle morphism."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We expect our constructions to converge to the corresponding objects in a \
suitable ",
 StyleBox["Gromov-Hausdorff limit",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " along sequences of graphs obtained by subdivision. We also want to show \
how graphs obtained by expansive rewriting give rise to space and continuous \
notions when aggregated at large observer scales."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

